    "case_summary": "A 43-year-old woman with mild intermittent asthma since childhood, well controlled on inhaled fluticasone and salmeterol. Her symptoms started 6 months before presentation, with dyspnea and chronic cough after resolution of a mild COVID-19 infection that didn't require hospitalization. She experienced progressive dyspnea, fatigue, anorexia, and weight loss. She was treated for an asthma exacerbation with a steroid taper without improvement. Seven days before presentation, she developed worsening shortness of breath and new right-sided, pleuritic chest pain.\n\nAt presentation:- Afebrile- Mildly tachypneic  - Oxygen saturation of 91% on room air\n\nLaboratory Tests:- Leukocyte count: 7.1 × 10⁹/L with 71% neutrophils and 0.1% eosinophils- Aspergillus IgG: >200 mg/L- A. niger IgE: 1.04 kU/L (weakly positive)- HIV antibody: negative- TB testing (interferon-gamma release assay): negative- Serum Aspergillus galactomannan index: <0.5 (negative)- Quantitative serum immunoglobulin: no immunoglobulin deficiencyImaging Studies:- Initial CT (5 months before presentation): Right upper-lobe opacities- Follow-up CT (3 months before presentation): Interval enlargement of parenchymal abnormality with ground-glass halo and progressive central cavitation- Admission CT: Right pneumothorax with interval progression of aspergilloma and suspected bronchopleural fistulaBronchoscopy/Pathology:- Bronchoalveolar lavage: Aspergillus niger isolated from right middle and lower lobe samples- Acid-fast bacilli smear and culture of BAL specimens: negative- Surgical pathology: Acute exudative pleuritis with pleural/subpleural abscess formation and degenerated hyphae on Grocott methenamine silver stain- Fungal and acid-fast bacillus cultures from surgical specimens: negative",
